## Research Context: Skill/Tool Creation — Focused Domain Research

You are conducting **focused domain research** for creating a skill or tool for the AI employee role: ****.

Your findings will be synthesized with local investigation results from other subtasks to produce a complete skill/tool specification.

**What you are researching**: A specific capability, workflow pattern, or domain concept that the skill/tool needs to implement. The description below tells you exactly what to focus on.

**What to produce**: Practical, actionable findings — concrete patterns, templates, frameworks, and best practices that can be directly used in a skill/tool specification. This is NOT an academic literature review.

**Source Guidance — Prioritize in this order:**
1. Official documentation for relevant platforms and tools mentioned in the task description
2. Industry-standard frameworks and methodologies (e.g., NIST AI RMF, ITIL, PMI, SAFe) — focus on the practical application, not theoretical foundations
3. Well-established best practices from recognized organizations — concrete templates, checklists, and workflow patterns
4. Real-world case studies with measurable outcomes

**Output Structure**: For each finding, provide:
- **What it is**: The pattern, template, framework, or best practice
- **How it works**: Concrete steps, fields, criteria, or structure
- **How it applies**: How this would be encoded in a skill (SKILL.md workflow guidance) or tool (executable operations)
- **Source**: Where you found it (URL, standard name, or organization)

**AI-Specific Considerations** — this skill/tool will be used by an AI employee:
- Focus on patterns that can be expressed as step-by-step workflows (good for SKILL.md)
- Identify where human confirmation should be required vs. autonomous execution
- Note any domain-specific terminology or taxonomies the AI needs to understand

## Original User Request
**Description**: Conduct external & codebase research for every capability targeted by the ML Lifecycle Execution Stack. For each of the 12 candidate deliverables (a)–(l) plus the named shared infrastructure (MLflow, ML Studio, Databricks, Tarot v3 ML Registry, Tecton/MLFS, MSP SDK, TEAMServe, Triton, SageMaker, Spinnaker, Micros, Bitbucket Pipelines, Optimus Prime, FinOps + ML Artifact Cost Monitoring, rovo-eval, AIX-eval), research authoritative APIs/SDKs/CLIs, design patterns, statistical/ML methodology, and existing related primary codebases. Concretely: (1) Training+HPO APIs and convergence-to-statistical-significance methodology; (2) MLflow tracking API patterns (run naming `Model_<TS>`, flattened params, per-segment metrics, nested runs); (3) Evaluation frameworks — rovo-eval/AIX-eval surfaces, multi-seed aggregation, k-fold/stratified, slice analysis (SliceLine/FairLearn), calibration (Platt/isotonic, ECE/Brier), fairness (EO/DPD), bootstrap CIs; (4) Error-analysis taxonomy literature (DQ vs model vs feature gap vs label noise vs distribution shift), detectors (CleanLab, KS/PSI); (5) Ablation matrix orchestration patterns (Hydra multirun, Optuna, MLflow nested runs); (6) Tecton SDK + MLFS client (FeatureView, materialization, online/offline join consistency); (7) Data-quality audit patterns (Great Expectations, Deequ, Soda — coverage/sparsity/freshness by connector/region/tenant); (8) MSP SDK packaging + Tarot v3 registration client + compliance-metadata schema; (9) Deployment manifest standards (model cards, Spinnaker pipeline, Micros descriptors, invariant/rollback DSLs); (10) Canary/shadow rollout APIs (TEAMServe, Triton, SageMaker production-variants), feature-flag gating, post-deploy invariant verification; (11) Documentation byproduct standards (model card per Mitchell, HuggingFace MC, architecture comparison, weekly digest, PIR frameworks); (12) Cost analyzer + SageMaker→TEAMServe migration calculator (FinOps queries, instance-class mapping, throughput-equivalence model). Use available code-search MCP tools (Bitbucket, rovodev) to discover internal codebases that wrap these APIs.

