# Research Report: Drift-Triggered Retraining Trigger Semantics (SOP-1Trigger Phase) and Hand-Off Contract

## Executive Summary

This research covers the drift-triggered retraining trigger semantics for the SRE/MLE AI employee role, focusing on how drift detection in ML Lens leads to automated retraining in ML Studio via StreamHub. The findings are derived from Atlassian's internal documentation on ML Platform services, drift detection architecture, circuit breaker patterns, and the responsible-ai-api production stack.

---

## 1. ML Lens Drift Detection — The Source of Trigger Signals

### What it is
ML Lens is Atlassian's centralized ML observability platform that computes drift metrics, emits alerts, and serves as the **trigger source** for retraining workflows. It supports multiple drift detection statistical tests.

### How it works — Drift Metrics Computed

| Metric | Feature Type | Computation | Threshold Guidance |
|--------|-------------|-------------|-------------------|
| KS statistic (D) | Numerical | Max CDF difference between current and reference windows | p-value ≤ 0.05 or D ≥ 0.1 |
| PSI (Population Stability Index) | Numerical | Binned log-ratio sum |≥ 0.1 (warning), ≥ 0.25 (critical) |
| Chi-squared | Categorical | Frequency distribution comparison | p-value ≤ 0.05 |
| Hellinger Distance | All (binned) | √(Σ(√p_i - √q_i)²) | Configurable per-feature |
| Cosine distance (for NLP/embeddings) | Embedding vectors | 1 - cosine_similarity(current, reference) | ≥ 0.40 distance (≤ 0.60 similarity) as floor |

**Source**: [Design: ML Lens Platform Data Drift Detection](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/6738511349/Design+ML+Lens+Platform+Data+Drift+Detection), [Option1: StarRocks as Compute Engine](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/6949715806/Option+1+StarRocks+as+Compute+Engine+Deep+Drift+Analysis), [H2 Action Plan: Model Observability](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/DTIE/pages/6495897543/H2+Action+Plan+Model+Observability+Performance)

### NLP-Specific Drift Floor (≥ 0.60 cosine similarity threshold)

For NLP/embedding-based models (like the responsible-ai-api's LLaMA moderation models), drift detection uses **embedding cosine similarity** comparison:
- A cosine similarity threshold of **≥ 0.60** is used as the NLP floor — below this, the drift is considered actionable
- This aligns with internal findings showing that cosine similarity ~0.879 already causes "meaningful reordering of close matches" in retrieval systems
- The agent recommendation system uses a0.6 cosine threshold for determining "no suitable agent" — establishing organizational precedent for this floor

**Source**: [SPIKE: Embedding model for agent recommendation](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/jai/pages/6627858662/SPIKE+Embedding+model+for+agent+recommendation), [FP32 to INT8 Embeddings Analysis](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/ae4/pages/6704627262/FP32+to+INT8+Embeddings+Analysis+Results+and+Reindexing+Decision), [Model drift identification and mitigation strategies](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/CA3/pages/1953707277/Model+drift+identification+and+mitigation+strategies)

### ML Lens Architecture (Preferred: Option 1 — StarRocks Direct)

```
Scheduler (every 5 min)→ Load drift configs from Postgres 
  → Issue SQL queries to StarRocks (parallel per feature) 
  → Read scalar results
  → KS: max(|cur_quantile_i - ref_quantile_i|) over100 pairs 
  → Threshold classification (DRIFT / WARNING / OK) 
  → Write results to feature_metrics table → Emit alert events if threshold breached (→ Slack / JSM Ops)
```

**Source**: [DACI: MLS vs ML Lens Drift Computation](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/6858051319/DACI+MLS+vs+ML+Lens+Drift+Computation)

