## 6. Circuit Breaker as Drift Guard — 20%/60s Window Pattern

### What it is
The ML-Engine Circuit Breaker Runbook defines the exact pattern referenced in the role document: a **20% failure rate threshold** over a **60-second rolling window** with a minimum of 20 calls before evaluation.

### How it works (from production ML-Engine config)

| Parameter | Value | Description |
|-----------|-------|-------------|
| `slidingWindowType` | TIME_BASED | Rolling time window |
| `slidingWindowSize` | 60 seconds | Window duration |
| `minimumNumberOfCalls` | 20 | Min calls before CB evaluates |
| `failureRateThreshold` | 20% | CB opens if failure rate ≥ 20% |
| `waitDurationInOpenState` | 30s base (exponential backoff to 60s max) | How long CB stays open |
| `permittedNumberOfCallsInHalfOpenState` | 20 | Trial probes in half-open |
| `automaticTransitionFromOpenToHalfOpenEnabled` | true | Auto-recovery |
| `exponentialBackoffMultiplier` | 2.0 | Backoff multiplier |
| `recordFailurePredicate` | Counts only5xx errors | What counts as failure |

### Relationship to drift-triggered retraining
When the circuit breaker **opens** due to model serving failures:
1. The CB open event is itself a **drift/degradation signal** that should be cross-correlated with drift metrics
2. If CB open correlates with detected feature/output drift → **strong confirmation for retraining trigger**
3. The fail-open kill-switch allows traffic to bypass moderation while retraining occurs

**Source**: [ML-Engine Circuit Breaker Runbook](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/ITSOL/pages/6058351626/ML-Engine+Circuit+Breaker+Runbook)

---

## 7. Responsible-AI-API Context — Production Retraining Triggers

### What it is
The responsible-ai-api uses a two-stage moderation pipeline (LLaMA 3.1 8B on TeamServe → GPT-4.1-mini via AI Gateway). Drift in moderation quality triggers retraining of the fine-tuned models.

### Current Production Model Stack

| Component | Model | Purpose |
|-----------|-------|---------|
| Prompt moderation | rai-ft-content-filter-v2-3-3 (LLaMA 3.1 8B via TeamServe) | Primary moderation |
| Agent moderation | GPT-4.1-mini (via AI Gateway) | Secondary/fallback |
| Circuit breaker (TeamServe) | `fail_max=30` | Triton gRPC CB |
| Fail-open behavior | When inference backend fails → ALLOWED (not DISALLOWED) | Production safety |

### HOT Incident Patterns That Indicate Drift/Degradation

From the AI-native SDLC page,8 HOT incidents in30 days revealed:
- Load Balancer / Timeout: 5 incidents → fixed by fail-open
- Agent Moderation Failed: 2 incidents → model migration needed
- TeamServe/GPU Issues: 1 incident → infrastructure

These patterns inform when the **SOP-1 trigger** should fire:
1. Sustained5xx from TeamServe (model serving degradation)
2. Elevated DISALLOWED rates without policy change (model quality drift)
3. Precision/recall degradation detected by MLE agent eval

**Source**: [AI-native SDLC: Responsible-AI-API](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~71202015faa243ae6c4091ade91d6c0353848f/pages/6808658401/AI-native+SDLC+Responsible-AI-API), [responsible-ai-api](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/TRUST/pages/5927717332/responsible-ai-api), [Runbook: Managing service nodes](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/RA2/pages/4803336656/Runbook+Managing+service+nodes+for+responsible-ai-api+service+during+incidents)

