## 6. Distribution Shift Branch (KS/PSI)

### 6.1 What It Is

Distribution shift occurs when the data the model encounters in production differs from the data it was trained on. Three sub-types:

| Type | What Shifts | Detection Method |
|------|-------------|-----------------|
| **Covariate Shift** | Input feature distributions P(X) | KS test, PSI on features |
| **Prior Probability Shift** | Label distribution P(Y) | Monitor class proportions |
| **Concept Drift** | Relationship P(Y|X) | Monitor model output + lagged evaluation |

### 6.2 Key Statistical Detectors

#### Population Stability Index (PSI)

**The industry standard** for monitoring feature drift at Atlassian:

```python
from atlassian_ml_dq_sdk.metrics.distribution import PopulationStabilityIndex

psi = PopulationStabilityIndex("feature", reference_distribution, bins=10)
psi_value = psi.compute(df)
```

**Interpretation thresholds:**
| PSI Value | Interpretation | Action |
|-----------|---------------|--------|
| < 0.1 | No significant shift | Normal |
| 0.1 - 0.25 | Moderate shift | Investigate |
| > 0.25 | Major shift | Retrain or recalibrate |

**How PSI works:**
1. Bin the data into N buckets
2. Calculate % in each bin for both reference and current
3. For each bin: `(current_% - reference_%) × ln(current_% / reference_%)`
4. Sum across all bins

**Implementation approaches at Atlassian:**
- **SQL (StarRocks)**: Using PERCENTILE_APPROX and basic operations
- **Kotlin**: Collect results and compute in-memory
- **Python/PySpark**: scipy with10% sampling, using numpy

**Source**: [Drift Detection Metrics](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/agd/pages/6192530763/Drift+Detection+Metrics), [Observo/Starrocks vs MLS Spike](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/6514084136/Observo+Starrocks+vs+MLS+Spike)

#### Kolmogorov-Smirnov (KS) Test

**The most powerful test for continuous distributions at large N:**

```python
from scipy.stats import ks_2samp

ks_statistic, p_value = ks_2samp(current_values, reference_values)
```

**Interpretation:**
- KS statistic > 0.2 → distribution drift detected
- p-value < 0.05 → statistically significant drift

**Configuration at Atlassian:**
- Conservative: `ks_threshold=0.05` (fewer alerts)
- Default: `ks_threshold=0.2`
- Sensitive: `ks_threshold=0.3` (more alerts)

**Source**: [Search Feature Observability - User Guide](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/3S/pages/5733362863/Search+Feature+Observability+-+User+Guide)

#### Statistical Test Auto-Selection (Atlassian PySparkAutoCalculator)

The Feature Drift Detection Architecture Proposal defines automatic test selection:

| Feature Type | Unique Values | Sample Size | Selected Test | Rationale |
|-------------|--------------|-------------|---------------|-----------|
| Numerical | Any | > 1000 | **KS** | Most powerful for continuous at large N |
| Numerical | Any | ≤ 1000 | **PSI (10bins)** | More stable at small N |
| Categorical | ≤ 50 unique | Any | **Chi-squared** | Standard for low cardinality |
| Categorical | > 50 unique | Any | **PSI (top-N bucketing)** | Chi-squared loses power at high cardinality |

**Source**: [Feature Drift Detection — Architecture Proposal](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/KYC/pages/6866709029/Feature+Drift+Detection+Architecture+Proposal)

#### Additional Methods

- **KL Divergence**: Asymmetric measure of distribution divergence
- **Wasserstein Distance**: Earth-mover distance — captures magnitude of shift
- **Jensen-Shannon Divergence (JSD)**: Symmetric variant of KL, range [0, 1]
- **Chi-squared Test**: For categorical features with moderate cardinality
- **Domain Classifiers**: Train a model to distinguish reference vs. current data; if easily separable, drift has occurred

### 6.3 Two-Stage Drift Detection Framework (Atlassian Best Practice)

The CoreML team's proposed approach:

**Stage 1 — Feature Drift Detection:**
Compute PSI for each feature, weighted by SHAP importance:
```
weighted_drift_score = Σ (PSI_i × normalized_shap_importance_i)
```

**Stage 2 — Output Drift Verification:**
If weighted_drift_score exceeds threshold, compute output distribution shift:
- Compare `prediction_distribution(current)` vs `prediction_distribution(reference)` using KS or PSI

**Alert Matrix:**
| Input Drift | Output Drift | Action |
|------------|-------------|--------|
| High | High | P0 — Likely model degradation. Investigate immediately. |
| High | Low | P1 — Model appears robust. Monitor closely. |
| Low | High | P0 — Concept drift or label shift. |
| Low | Low | Normal — No action required. |

**Source**: [H2 Action Plan: Model Observability & Performance](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/DTIE/pages/6495897543/H2+Action+Plan+Model+Observability+Performance)

### 6.4 Reference Windows

| Window | Description | Use Case |
|--------|-------------|----------|
| Training baseline | Distribution at time of model training | Long-term structural drift |
| Rolling 30-day | Trailing 30 days of production data | Short-term operational drift |
| Same-period prior year | Seasonal comparison | Seasonality-adjusted drift |

### 6.5 Drift Metrics Storage Schema (Atlassian Standard)

```
core_ml_data.coreml_drift_metrics
├── ds (STRING)
├── pipeline_name (STRING)
├── model_name (STRING)
├── feature_name (STRING) — NULL for output-level drift
├── drift_test (STRING) — "psi", "ks", "jsd", "chi2", "wasserstein"
├── drift_value (DOUBLE)
├── drift_threshold_warning (DOUBLE)
├── drift_threshold_error (DOUBLE)
├── reference_window (STRING) — "training", "30d_rolling", "yoy"
├── feature_importance_tier (STRING)
├── status (STRING)
```

**Source**: [H2 Action Plan: Model Observability & Performance](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/DTIE/pages/6495897543/H2+Action+Plan+Model+Observability+Performance)

### 6.6 How It Applies to SKILL.md

The AI employee should:
1. Auto-select statistical test based on feature type and sample size
2. Compute drift metrics against multiple reference windows (training, 30d rolling, seasonal)
3. Weight by feature importance (SHAP) for alert prioritization
4. Apply two-stage validation: input drift → output drift correlation
5. Classify drift severity and route to appropriate action
6. Store metrics in standardized schema for monitoring

