## 6. Ablation Study Patterns — Established Methodology

### What it is
An ablation study systematically removes or disables components to measure each component's marginal contribution.

### Internal Example — User Signal Ablation

From Atlassian's search relevance team:

| Feature Configuration | Recall@20| Recall@40 | Recall@60 | MRR |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| A. Query + Doc features (baseline) | 0.800 | 0.893 | 0.921 | 0.452 |
| B. UserProfile-PRS ON | 0.811 | 0.893 | 0.919 | 0.460 |
| C. User view history ON | 0.808 | 0.889 | 0.914 | 0.445 |
| D. Social affinity ON | 0.809 | 0.898 | 0.921 | 0.445 |
| E. All ON + extra doc features | 0.814 | 0.885 | 0.919 | 0.452 |

[Source: 2025-08Ablation Study for User signal](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/jirasearch/pages/5661423576)

### Feature Selection via Ablation (L1 Model)

The Search Relevance team runs systematic ablation for feature selection:
- Baseline: 13 features, `max_depth: 4, n_estimators: 600`
- Top-12feature combinations compared against baseline
- Each run gets separate hyperparameter tuning
- Results tracked across4 evaluation sets (clicked, failed queries, human curated, linked docs)

[Source: Round4 of L1 model experiment](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SRCA/pages/6036282195)

### How it applies to the skill/tool

The ablation matrix orchestrator should support:
1. **Leave-one-out ablation**: Remove each feature/component individually
2. **Additive ablation**: Start from minimal and add one-at-a-time
3. **Combinatorial ablation**: Test all 2^n or factorial combinations
4. **Partial factorial**: Use Optuna/Bayesian to explore the most informative subset

