## 7. Autoresearch Loop — Agentic Experiment Iteration

### What it is
An internal Rovo Dev skill (`autoresearch-loop`) that composes existing tools for autonomous hyperparameter mutation and evaluation.

### How it works
```
READ program.md + results.tsv
    ↓
PROPOSE one hyperparameter mutation (e.g. bump matrix_lr by 25%)
    ↓
atlas ml workflow run -d autoresearch_experiment.yaml -e prod --open-url
    ↓
mlexp log --yaml ... --blueprint-id ... --run-id ... --notes "..."
    ↓
mlexp track --latest --interval 30(polls status every 30s)
    ↓
mlexp fetch-mlflow <run_id> --attach<mlexp_id>(pulls val_bpb)
    ↓
KEEP (advance) or DISCARD (git revert HEAD)
    ↓
NEVER STOP
```

[Source: Autoresearch on ML Studio — A Hacker's Field Guide](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~712020d2e2445580da4d86b9e6a5e346190367/pages/6848133680)

### How it applies to the skill/tool

This is the most directly relevant pattern for an AI employee ablation tool:
- AI proposes next ablation configuration
- Launches ML Studio workflow
- Tracks progress
- Fetches results
- Decides whether to continue or revert
- Loops indefinitely until convergence or budget exhaustion

---

## 8. Tenant Embedding FT — Sweep Pattern

### What it is
A concrete hyperparameter sweep implementation using YAML-based configuration per experiment.

### How it works
```
Code change (branch `tenant-embedding-combined-ft-eval-workflow`):
    - Combined workflow: tenant_embedding_ft_eval_workflow.yaml
    - Sweep YAMLs: hparam_search/combined/expNN_<label>.yaml (6 files)
    - Runner: run_all.sh
    - Validator: validate.sh
    - Collector: collect_results.py
```

Six experiments with controlled parameter variations:
| exp | Configuration | run_id |
|-----|---------------|--------|
| exp01_lower_lr (**winner**) | lr=1e-5 | def2568c... |
| exp02_higher_lr | lr=3e-5 | 7f4d058f... |
| exp03_more_epochs | epochs=5 | 353847cd... |
| exp04_smaller_eff_bs | batch_size=8 | 0a4bd0d4... |
| exp05_higher_lr_warmup | lr=5e-5+warmup | 830febdb... |
| exp06_low_decay | weight_decay=1e-4 | e8ec06dd... |

[Source: Tenant embedding FT - Hyperparameter sweep](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~712020b392f1b430fd427da35485751be61328/pages/6864477381)

### How it applies to the skill/tool

This shows the practical internal convention:
- Each experiment gets its own YAML config file (`expNN_<label>.yaml`)
- A shell script (`run_all.sh`) launches all experiments
- A validator checks results
- A collector aggregates results into a comparison table
- Winner is promoted to production defaults

---

## 9. Synthesis — Recommended Orchestration Architecture for SKILL.md

### Architecture for the Ablation Matrix Tool

```
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                  ABLATION MATRIX ORCHESTRATOR             │
├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│1. DEFINE: Hydra config groups for each ablation axis│
│    - features: [user_signals, social, freshness, ...]    │
│    - models: [xgboost, lightgbm, neural]│
│    - data: [full, sampled_10k, stratified]               │
│                │
│ 2. PLAN: Generate ablation matrix                        │
│    - Full factorial (2^n) OR                             │
│    - Optuna-guided partial exploration OR                │
│    - Leave-one-out pattern                               │
│                                                          │
│ 3. EXECUTE: Parallel launch via Databricks Jobs API      │
│    - Each config → ML Studio workflow run│
│    - Track via mlexp CLI                                 │
│    - MLflow nested runs (parent = matrix, child = cell)  │
│                                                          │
│ 4. COLLECT: Aggregate results                            │
│    - Per-cell metrics from MLflow child runs             │
│    - compute_results.py → comparison DataFrame           │
│    - Optuna param_importances for ranking                │
│                                                          │
│ 5. ANALYZE: Statistical significance + visualization│
│    - Bootstrap CIs on metric deltas                      │
│    - plot_param_importances (fANOVA)                     │
│    - plot_contour / plot_parallel_coordinate             │
│                                                          │
│ 6. DECIDE: Human confirmation before promotion│
│    - Present ranked feature contributions                │
│    - Flag statistically insignificant differences│
│    - Recommend winner config│
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

### Key Design Decisions for AI Employee Execution

| Decision | Autonomous | Requires Human Confirmation |
|----------|:---:|:---:|
| Generate ablation matrix configs | ✅ | |
| Launch sweep jobs | ✅ | |
| Monitor and collect results | ✅ | |
| Compute statistics and visualizations | ✅ | |
| Prune clearly underperforming trials | ✅ | |
| Promote winning config to production | |✅ |
| Decide on budget extension | | ✅ |
| Select evaluation metrics | | ✅ (first time) |

### Domain Terminology the AI Must Understand

- **Ablation**: Systematic removal of components to measure marginal contribution
- **Leave-one-out**: Remove one feature at a time, compare to full baseline
- **Feature importance via fANOVA**: Functional ANOVA decomposition of variance (what Optuna's `plot_param_importances` uses)
- **TPE (Tree Parzen Estimator)**: Optuna's default Bayesian optimization algorithm
- **MedianPruner**: Early stopping of unpromising trials based on intermediate results
- **Nested run**: MLflow parent-child hierarchy for organizing sweeps
- **Config group**: Hydra concept — a folder of alternative YAML configs for a single dimension
- **Override**: Hydra concept — CLI or file-based parameter override: `model.lr=0.01`
- **Multirun**: Hydra mode that launches multiple jobs from a single command

