# Subtask-1: Association Map — Existing Tools vs. Missing Capabilities

## Executive Summary

This research maps existing Atlassian platform tools (JSM Ops, Slack MCP, Bitbucket, ML Lens, SignalFx) against the eight capabilities required for the Production Reliability + AI Gateway Ops stack. For each capability gap, I provide justification for a new tool or skill, grounded in the actual codebase, documentation, and operational patterns discovered in the organization.

---

## 1. Existing Tools — Current Capabilities Inventory

### 1.1 JSM Ops (Jira Service Management Operations)

**What it provides today:**
- Alert ingestion from monitoring tools (SignalFx, Datadog, CloudWatch, Prometheus)
- On-call management (schedules, escalation policies, rotations)
- Incident lifecycle management (create, acknowledge, escalate, resolve)
- Alert grouping and deduplication
- Integration with OpsGenie for paging
- Automation rules (A4J) for triggered actions on alert events
- REST API for alert CRUD, incident management, and on-call queries
- AI-powered: similar incident surfacing, automated PIR creation, suggestion panel

**What it CANNOT do (gaps relevant to items a–h):**
- Cannot query ML Lens drift metrics or model performance dashboards
- Cannot author or deploy SignalFx detectors-as-code
- Cannot trigger model retraining pipelines
- Cannot manipulate circuit breaker state or rate-limit configurations
- Cannot execute the30-min status cadence with cross-correlation (needs custom orchestration)
- Cannot manage per-tenant rate-limit token buckets directly
- Cannot configure AI Gateway routing policies

**Source:** [JSM Ops / OpsGenie overview](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/ITSOL/pages/1528653550), [Incident Response](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~7120202eb77464acc74fafa31e8e81f0b11763/pages/6159048540)

---

### 1.2 Slack MCP (Message Control Protocol)

**What it provides today:**
- Send messages to channels (including structured blocks)
- Read channel history and thread replies
- Search messages across workspace
- User lookup

**What it CANNOT do:**
- Cannot acknowledge a page/alert in JSM Ops via Slack action
- Cannot post structured incident status updates with templated 30-min cadence
- Cannot cross-correlate alerts with recent deployments, changes, and drift signals
- Cannot classify severity from incident context
- Cannot trigger escalation workflows

**Source:** Slack MCP toolset in the agent's available tools

---

### 1.3 Bitbucket

**What it provides today:**
- Repository browsing and code search
- Pull request management (create, review, comment)
- Pipeline/build status queries
- Commit and branch operations
- Deployment pipeline queries

**What it CANNOT do:**
- Cannot generate or validate Terraform HCL for SignalFx detectors
- Cannot interpret or validate `.sauron.yml` configurations
- Cannot run `sfm plan` or trigger Sauron pipeline steps
- Cannot generate per-endpoint SLO definitions in IaC

**Source:** BitbucketAgent capabilities, [Sauron documentation](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/ATF/pages/1059524356)

---

### 1.4 ML Lens (Platform)

**What it provides today:**
- Metric store (StarRocks/Observo) with time-series drift metrics
- Query API over HTTP for fetching feature data
- Drift detection: KS test (D-statistic, p-value), PSI, summary statistics
- Alert evaluation engine with configurable thresholds (namespace `feature_observability.drift.*`)
- Alert routing to Slack and JSM Ops
- Feature discovery across MLFS-registered feature services
- Data quality profiling (null%, mean, stddev)
- ML Studio integration for batch drift computation jobs

**What it CANNOT do (for the SRE AI Employee):**
- No existing API/tool that the AI employee can call to query drift metrics on-demand (needs a wrapper tool)
- No cosine-similarity or KL-divergence computation for NLP embeddings currently in platform (KS and PSI only)
- Cannot surface a unified "data-quality / model-perf / system-health" dashboard view programmatically
- Cannot trigger retraining based on drift (RFC exists but not implemented)
- No≥0.60 NLP floor threshold configured by default

**Source:** [ML Lens Platform Data Drift Detection](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/6738511349), [ML Lens One Pager](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/5362077533), [DACI: MLS vs ML Lens Drift Computation](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/6858051319), [Drift Detection Metrics](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/agd/pages/6192530763)

---

### 1.5 SignalFx (Splunk Observability Cloud)

**What it provides today:**
- Real-time metrics ingestion and dashboards
- Detectors (alert rules) with SignalFlow query language
- Integration with JSM Ops/OpsGenie for paging
- SLO monitoring (latency, error rate, availability)
- Terraform provider (`signalfx_detector`, `signalfx_dashboard`)
- Sauron deployment pipeline for IaC (auto-apply on merge to master)

**What it CANNOT do (for the SRE AI Employee):**
- Cannot author detectors autonomously (needs a tool that generates valid Terraform HCL)
- Cannot encode per-endpoint SLO definitions (this must be done via Tome + Terraform)
- Cannot validate detector thresholds against current baseline noise
- No existing tool for the AI to read current detector state and compare against desired state

**Source:** [SignalFX overview](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/TRUST/pages/6422841929), [OPP-202605-04](https://hello.jira.atlassian.cloud/browse/AI-264), [RFC: Maintain Team SFX Dashboards & Detectors as Code](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/afm/pages/6101950951)

---

## 2. Capability Gap Analysis — Justification for New Tools/Skills

### (a) ML Lens Query Tool — Drift Metrics

| Aspect | Detail |
|--------|--------|
| **Gap** | No existing MCP tool wraps the ML Lens Query API or Metrics API for on-demand queries. The AI employee cannot currently ask "What is the KS drift score for feature X in the last 24h?" |
| **What exists** | ML Lens has an HTTP Query API (StarRocks-backed), a Metrics API, and alert evaluation. But these are internal service APIs with no agent-facing tool. |
| **Missing metric types** | KL divergence and cosine similarity for NLP embeddings are not yet computed by ML Lens (only KS and PSI). The ≥0.60 NLP floor needs custom thresholds. |
| **Justification** | A new **tool** is needed that wraps the ML Lens Query API to: (1) query drift metrics by namespace/feature/window, (2) apply NLP-specific thresholds (cosine ≥0.60), (3) return structured dashboard-like summaries (data-quality, model-perf, system-health). |
| **Human confirmation** | Read-only queries — no confirmation needed. |
| **Source** | [ML Lens Query API](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/5937111395), [Option 1: StarRocks Deep Drift Analysis](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/6949715806) |

---

### (b) SignalFx Detectors-as-Code Authoring Tool + Per-Endpoint SLOs

| Aspect | Detail |
|--------|--------|
| **Gap** | No tool generates valid Terraform HCL for `signalfx_detector` resources or Tome SLO definitions. Currently0 detectors exist in the responsible-ai-api IaC (`grep -c "signalfx_detector" operations/terraform/*.tf == 0`). |
| **What exists** | Sauron pipeline deploys Terraform on merge to master. SignalFM exports existing detectors. But no tool *authors new detectors* from specifications. |
| **Justification** | A new **tool** is needed that: (1) generates `signalfx_detector` Terraform HCL from a detector specification (metric, threshold, duration, severity, notifications), (2) generates Tome SLO capability definitions, (3) validates against SignalFlow syntax, (4) creates PRs to the operations/terraform/ directory. |
| **Human confirmation** | REQUIRED — PR review before merge (Sauron only applies on master). |
| **Source** | [OPP-202605-04](https://hello.jira.atlassian.cloud/browse/AI-264), [STRA-202605-06](https://hello.jira.atlassian.cloud/browse/AI-264), [Sauron SignalFX Howto](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/ATF/pages/1059524356) |

---

### (c) Drift-Triggered Retraining Trigger Skill (SOP-1)

| Aspect | Detail |
|--------|--------|
| **Gap** | ML Lens has alerting but no automated retraining trigger. The RFC for "Model Re-training using Data Quality and ML Lens" exists but is not implemented. |
| **What exists** | ML Lens can evaluate drift thresholds and emit alerts. ML Studio can run training workflows. But the linkage (drift alert → retraining job trigger) is manual today. |
| **Justification** | A new **skill** (SKILL.md workflow) is needed that encodes the SOP-1 trigger phase: (1) receive drift alert (KS/PSI/cosine above threshold), (2) validate alert is sustained (not transient), (3) check if retraining is already in-flight, (4) trigger ML Studio retraining blueprint via API, (5) post status to Slack, (6) create tracking Jira issue. |
| **Human confirmation** | REQUIRED at step4 — retraining has cost and risk implications. |
| **Source** | [RFC: Model Re-training using Data Quality and ML Lens](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/6013257858) |

---

### (d) Circuit-Breaker Controller Skill + Tool (20%/60s window, fail-open kill-switch)

| Aspect | Detail |
|--------|--------|
| **Gap** | The responsible-ai-api has circuit breakers on prompt/output moderation but NOT on agent moderation. No existing tool can read CB state, toggle kill-switches, or configure thresholds. |
| **What exists** | Feature gates in `feature_service.py` (e.g., `should_fail_open_on_model_timeout()`, `should_fail_open_if_circuit_breaker_open()`). Resilience4j-style patterns. Kill-switch gates (default-off). The codebase uses `inference_error_handler()` context manager. |
| **Justification** | Two components needed: (1) A **tool** that can read/write circuit breaker configuration (threshold=20%, window=60s, current state), toggle the fail-open kill-switch gate, and emit the `agent_moderation_fail_open` metric status. (2) A **skill** encoding the SOP for when to engage/disengage the CB and kill-switch (automated vs. human-confirmed scenarios). |
| **Human confirmation** | Kill-switch toggle: REQUIRED. CB threshold adjustment: REQUIRED. Reading CB state: autonomous. |
| **Source** | [AI-206: Add fail-open/circuit breaker to agent moderation](https://hello.jira.atlassian.cloud/browse/AI-206), [OPP-202605-02](https://hello.jira.atlassian.cloud/browse/AI-266) |

---

### (e) Incident Response SOP-3 Skill

| Aspect | Detail |
|--------|--------|
| **Gap** | No encoded, AI-executable incident response workflow exists. Current incident response is manual with ad-hoc Slack coordination. The15-minute on-call standard exists but there's no automation for the full SOP-3 lifecycle. |
| **What exists** | JSM Ops creates HOT tickets and pages on-call. Slack channels are created. The 15-Minute Rule standard exists. But severity classification, cross-correlation with recent deploys/drift/changes, and 30-min status cadence are all manual. |
| **Justification** | A new **skill** (SKILL.md) is needed encoding: (1) Slack acknowledgement within X minutes, (2) severity classification using signal correlation (metrics + recent deploys + drift alerts), (3) cross-correlation engine (query SignalFx + ML Lens + Bitbucket recent deploys), (4) 30-minute status update cadence with templated updates, (5) escalation decision tree (if not mitigated in N minutes → page next tier). |
| **Human confirmation** | Severity classification: autonomous (but human can override). Escalation: REQUIRED confirmation. Status updates: autonomous after initial humanack. |
| **Source** | [Official On-Call Standard](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/IMPIR/pages/5046067535), [CSS Incident SOP](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/GEM/pages/5678891272), [Incident Response](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~7120202eb77464acc74fafa31e8e81f0b11763/pages/6159048540) |

