## Executive Summary

This research maps existing Atlassian platform tools (JSM Ops, Slack MCP, Bitbucket, ML Lens, SignalFx) against the eight capabilities required for the Production Reliability + AI Gateway Ops stack. For each capability gap, I provide justification for a new tool or skill, grounded in the actual codebase, documentation, and operational patterns discovered in the organization.

---

## 1. Existing Tools — Current Capabilities Inventory

### 1.1 JSM Ops (Jira Service Management Operations)

**What it provides today:**
- Alert ingestion from monitoring tools (SignalFx, Datadog, CloudWatch, Prometheus)
- On-call management (schedules, escalation policies, rotations)
- Incident lifecycle management (create, acknowledge, escalate, resolve)
- Alert grouping and deduplication
- Integration with OpsGenie for paging
- Automation rules (A4J) for triggered actions on alert events
- REST API for alert CRUD, incident management, and on-call queries
- AI-powered: similar incident surfacing, automated PIR creation, suggestion panel

**What it CANNOT do (gaps relevant to items a–h):**
- Cannot query ML Lens drift metrics or model performance dashboards
- Cannot author or deploy SignalFx detectors-as-code
- Cannot trigger model retraining pipelines
- Cannot manipulate circuit breaker state or rate-limit configurations
- Cannot execute the30-min status cadence with cross-correlation (needs custom orchestration)
- Cannot manage per-tenant rate-limit token buckets directly
- Cannot configure AI Gateway routing policies

**Source:** [JSM Ops / OpsGenie overview](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/ITSOL/pages/1528653550), [Incident Response](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~7120202eb77464acc74fafa31e8e81f0b11763/pages/6159048540)

---

### 1.2 Slack MCP (Message Control Protocol)

**What it provides today:**
- Send messages to channels (including structured blocks)
- Read channel history and thread replies
- Search messages across workspace
- User lookup

**What it CANNOT do:**
- Cannot acknowledge a page/alert in JSM Ops via Slack action
- Cannot post structured incident status updates with templated 30-min cadence
- Cannot cross-correlate alerts with recent deployments, changes, and drift signals
- Cannot classify severity from incident context
- Cannot trigger escalation workflows

**Source:** Slack MCP toolset in the agent's available tools

---

### 1.3 Bitbucket

**What it provides today:**
- Repository browsing and code search
- Pull request management (create, review, comment)
- Pipeline/build status queries
- Commit and branch operations
- Deployment pipeline queries

**What it CANNOT do:**
- Cannot generate or validate Terraform HCL for SignalFx detectors
- Cannot interpret or validate `.sauron.yml` configurations
- Cannot run `sfm plan` or trigger Sauron pipeline steps
- Cannot generate per-endpoint SLO definitions in IaC

**Source:** BitbucketAgent capabilities, [Sauron documentation](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/ATF/pages/1059524356)

---

### 1.4 ML Lens (Platform)

**What it provides today:**
- Metric store (StarRocks/Observo) with time-series drift metrics
- Query API over HTTP for fetching feature data
- Drift detection: KS test (D-statistic, p-value), PSI, summary statistics
- Alert evaluation engine with configurable thresholds (namespace `feature_observability.drift.*`)
- Alert routing to Slack and JSM Ops
- Feature discovery across MLFS-registered feature services
- Data quality profiling (null%, mean, stddev)
- ML Studio integration for batch drift computation jobs

**What it CANNOT do (for the SRE AI Employee):**
- No existing API/tool that the AI employee can call to query drift metrics on-demand (needs a wrapper tool)
- No cosine-similarity or KL-divergence computation for NLP embeddings currently in platform (KS and PSI only)
- Cannot surface a unified "data-quality / model-perf / system-health" dashboard view programmatically
- Cannot trigger retraining based on drift (RFC exists but not implemented)
- No≥0.60 NLP floor threshold configured by default

**Source:** [ML Lens Platform Data Drift Detection](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/6738511349), [ML Lens One Pager](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/5362077533), [DACI: MLS vs ML Lens Drift Computation](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/6858051319), [Drift Detection Metrics](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/agd/pages/6192530763)

---

### 1.5 SignalFx (Splunk Observability Cloud)

**What it provides today:**
- Real-time metrics ingestion and dashboards
- Detectors (alert rules) with SignalFlow query language
- Integration with JSM Ops/OpsGenie for paging
- SLO monitoring (latency, error rate, availability)
- Terraform provider (`signalfx_detector`, `signalfx_dashboard`)
- Sauron deployment pipeline for IaC (auto-apply on merge to master)

**What it CANNOT do (for the SRE AI Employee):**
- Cannot author detectors autonomously (needs a tool that generates valid Terraform HCL)
- Cannot encode per-endpoint SLO definitions (this must be done via Tome + Terraform)
- Cannot validate detector thresholds against current baseline noise
- No existing tool for the AI to read current detector state and compare against desired state

**Source:** [SignalFX overview](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/TRUST/pages/6422841929), [OPP-202605-04](https://hello.jira.atlassian.cloud/browse/AI-264), [RFC: Maintain Team SFX Dashboards & Detectors as Code](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/afm/pages/6101950951)

