                                 Key findings

                   Two layers of "existing" coverage exist

 1 Currently installed (/Users/tchen7/.agents/skills/,
   src/openteam/server/resources/skills/, MCP toolsets): no ML primitives.
   Only twg, jira, confluence, research, full-context-mode, plus generic
   Atlassian/Bitbucket/Compass/Switcheroo/Slack/Socrates/etc. None wrap
   MLflow, ML Studio, Optimus Prime, rovo-eval, AIX-eval, or Tarot v3.
 2 Prior role_setup runs in
   _runtime/tasks/role_setup/role_setup_20260524_072327_* and
   _20260524_081951_* already produced machine-readable tool.json specs and 
   SKILL.md files for the AI MLE problem — these are the authoritative
   starting point. Run B's TOOLS_CATALOGUE.md enumerates 13 ML-platform tools
   and 7 skills.

                        Per-candidate coverage vs. gap

 • (a) Training/HPO — Prior ml-studio-job spec covers submit, hpo_sweep (with
   explicit optimus_prime strategy + L-band budget caps L2≤50…L5∞),
   convergence_diag. Gaps: "statistical" (not plateau) convergence test,
   batch_submit for parallel architectures, required reproducibility tags,
   multi-backend selector for Databricks/ML Fabric.
 • (b) MLflow — Prior mlflow-run spec directly encodes the three §4.3 
   conventions (Model_<TS> regex, params_must_be_flat=true, slice arg on
   log_metric, seed-required). Gaps: log_model(framework,…), parent_run_id
   arg, aggregate_seeds helper writing canonical _lower_ci/_upper_ci suffixes,
   log_failure_taxonomy op.
 • (c) Eval (rovo+AIX) — Most thoroughly designed candidate. eval-runner tool
   merges rovo-eval + AIX-eval + Stratus with framework selector; native
   seeds[] and kfold_k params; unified result schema including per_seed,
   aggregate.{mean,std,ci_lower,ci_upper}, slices, fairness,
   calibration.{ece,brier}, failure_taxonomy. ml-evaluation-rigor skill
   encodes 10 invariants (multi-seed, k-fold, slice, calibration, CIs,
   fairness, ablation interactions, every-failure-categorised, no leakage,
   pre-analysis frozen). ml-evaluation-elevated-standards skill encodes the
   6-stage protocol as refuse-to-emit gate. Gaps: compare(eval_ids[]),
   red_team mode, judge≠candidate enforcement at tool level,
   replay_production_traffic source helper.
 • (e) Ablation matrix — True green-field gap. No dedicated tool; ablation is
   workflow-encoded inside ml-experiment-lifecycle step 5 as multiple
   ml-studio-job.submit calls. Missing: declarative DSL, interaction-effect
   attribution (Sobol), baseline-reuse hook, structured ablation_report
   artifact.

                      Cross-cutting patterns to inherit

 • All Run-B tools use shared_base: ml_client_base (ASAP auth, audit,
   rate-limit defaults).
 • Every mutating op MUST call symphony-autonomy-check.validate (global
   invariant).
 • Every audit event MUST be bound via orbit-context-bind.bind_audit_event for
   ORBIT lineage.
 • Rigor enforcement is a RigorViolation exception path from
   ml-evaluation-rigor — new tools must integrate with it or be bypassed.

                 Domain Operational Artifacts (scope a/b/c/e)

 • Runbooks: ML Runbook template covered by ml-runbook-authoring; ML Launch
   Review Checklist G6 wired to eval-runner+ml-evaluation-rigor.
 • Eval frameworks: rovo-eval / AIX-eval / Stratus all unified under
   eval-runner with §10.2 elevated standards encoded as I1–I10 invariants.
 • §4.3 MLflow patterns: 4 of 5 covered; log_model is the one gap.
 • §4.4 templates: experiment report, architecture comparison report, model
   card — orchestration exists but no first-class generator tool (overlap with
   candidate k).

                                Recommendation


Treat Run B's TOOLS_CATALOGUE.md as the authoritative starting point. New
tools should be justified as deltas against it (the 4+4+5 specific gaps for
a/b/c plus the dedicated ablation tool for e), not as green-field builds —
preserving the parent-task mandate that "each new tool MUST justify why
existing tools are insufficient."
