## 1. Canary/Shadow Rollout APIs

### 1.1 TEAMServe (Atlassian Internal Model-as-a-Service)

**What it is**: TEAMServe is Atlassian's internal "model-as-a-service" platform built on Kubernetes (GKE/EKS) + ArgoCD. It supports training, packaging, hosting, and serving ML/LLM models on managed GPU infrastructure. It runs runtimes including `triton-server`, `vllm-server`, and `trtllm-serve`.

**How canary/shadow works on TEAMServe**:
- **Shadow testing pattern**: Dual-invoke both old (SageMaker) and new (TEAMServe) endpoints simultaneously; log shadow results to compare quality/latency without affecting user-visible responses. Feature flags gate which path returns the response to caller.
- **Regional rollout cadence**: TEAMServe enforces a formalized regional rollout cadence across `us-east-1`, `us-west-2`, `eu-central-1`, `eu-west-*`.
- **GitOps deployment**: Deployments via Kustomize overlay + `application.yaml`, synced through ArgoCD. Endpoint URL stability is maintained via a generated file in Bitbucket (not the live API) to avoid cascading failures during rollouts.
- **Multi-model packing for canary**: TEAMServe supports changing per-tenant L2 models from single to multi-model packed configuration, enabling model rollout, test flight, and subsequent old model removal on the same compute without separate GPU capacity planning.

**Key APIs/Clients**:
- `TeamservePassageRerankingGrpcClient` — gRPC client for passage reranking models
- `TeamserveAllMpnetBaseV2EmbeddingClient` — embedding model client
- `TritonGrpcChannelFactory` → `ManagedChannel` — Triton gRPC channel
- HTTP POST to `/v1/models/<name>/infer` — HTTP inference path
- V2 form: `/v2/<team>/<model>/infer`

**How it applies to skill/tool**:
- The AI employee should be able to orchestrate shadow tests by configuring dual-invoke patterns: invoke both old and new endpoints, compare outputs (error rate, MAE, latency percentiles), and emit metrics.
- Workflow steps: (1) Deploy model to TEAMServe via Kustomize PR; (2) Configure shadow flag; (3) Run dual-invoke for N hours; (4) Analyze quality parity metrics; (5) Gate cutover decision.
- Human confirmation required: Cutover from shadow to production traffic.

**Source**: [TEAMServe Regional Processing Enforcement](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~7120200d11664033264b52904fa98ad4dac86a/pages/6974086089), [Comments & Extensions: RFC Hardened TEAMServe ↔ Service Integration](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~7120203e8fad871e7f452c8bec69de58960c46/pages/7031701479), [RFC: Streamlining Search →3S → TeamServe 200Tenants model deployment process](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~712020c499e755dcd84e699331f8f9e7533a28/pages/6995883643)

---

### 1.2 NVIDIA Triton Inference Server

**What it is**: Triton is the primary inference runtime on TEAMServe. It supports multi-framework models (TensorFlow, PyTorch, TensorRT, ONNX), dynamic model management, and ensemble pipelines.

**How canary/shadow works with Triton**:
- **Model versioning**: Triton supports model versioning with version policies (latest, specific, all). New model versions can be loaded without restarting the server.
- **Ensemble pipelines**: Multi-model DAG pipelines allow routing through pre-processing → inference → post-processing. Business Logic Scripting (BLS) enables custom control flow within Python models.
- **Config-driven ensembles**: A config-driven Triton server proposal abstracts model implementations to YAML, allowing easier A/B swaps of model components within an ensemble.
- **Dynamic batching**: Optimizes throughput for canary traffic (which may be low-volume).

**Key configuration patterns**:
```
model-name/
├── config.pbtxt          # Model config (backend, inputs, outputs, batching)
├── 1/# Version1
│   ├── model.py# Python backend
│   └── model.onnx       # Weights
├── 2/                    # Version 2 (canary)
│   ├── model.py
│   └── model.onnx
```

**Endpoint routing for A/B**:
- TEAMServe routing: `/v1/models/ml-registry/<model-group>/versions/<version>/v2/models/<ensemble>/infer`
- Searcher configs + Statsig experiment controls which model version receives traffic.

**How it applies to skill/tool**:
- The skill should handle: (1) packaging new model version into Triton-compatible directory structure; (2) deploying new version alongside existing; (3) configuring version policy; (4) routing via external feature flag.
- Autonomous execution: Model version deployment to Triton. Human confirmation: Traffic routing decisions.

**Source**: [Nvidia Triton Inference Server Notes](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~7120209020b619f01f4dfba157f28e77d829b0/pages/6782108882), [Config Driven Triton Server Proposal](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~7120205ec90d24e1e64d6b9eef5f0f932a4f5f/pages/6304700128), [Run book: Packaging and Deploying HuggingFace ML Models to Serving Endpoints in TeamServe](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/jai/pages/6675269771)

---

### 1.3 AWS SageMaker Production & Shadow Variants

**What it is**: SageMaker supports two types of variants behind an endpoint: **production variants** (serve real traffic) and **shadow variants** (replicate traffic for comparison without returning responses to callers).

**How it works**:
- **Production Variants**: Multiple models behind one endpoint, each with configurable `InitialVariantWeight` for traffic split. Each variant can have different instance types and autoscaling policies.
- **Shadow Variants**: A portion of inference requests to production variants is replicated to shadow variants. Shadow responses are logged to S3 for comparison, never returned to caller.
- **Blue-green deployment**: SageMaker supports updating endpoints via new `EndpointConfig` with canary/gradual rollout options.

**Key API patterns**:
```python
# Production variants for A/B
sm_client.create_endpoint_config(
    EndpointConfigName="config",
    ProductionVariants=[
        {'VariantName': 'ModelA', 'InitialVariantWeight': 0.9, ...},
        {'VariantName': 'ModelB', 'InitialVariantWeight': 0.1, ...}
    ],
    ShadowProductionVariants=[
        {'VariantName': 'ShadowModel', 'SamplingPercentage': 50, ...}
    ]
)
```

**Limitations at Atlassian** (being deprecated):
- SageMaker is being actively migrated to TEAMServe (~59% cost savings, ~$5.6M/year).
- Production Variants don't support dynamic Statsig-based traffic overrides without config redeployment.
- No built-in caching/model-unloading like Triton MME.

**How it applies to skill/tool**:
- For legacy SageMaker models not yet migrated, the skill should support creating shadow variant configurations and analyzing S3-logged shadow responses.
- For new models, prefer TEAMServe + feature-flag gating over SageMaker variants.
- The migration calculator tool should map SageMaker variant configs to equivalent TEAMServe multi-model packed configurations.

**Source**: [Production and Shadow Variants: Explore](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/3606681077), [RFC: Use Production Variants to Implement Multi Model Endpoints](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/3S/pages/4000955365), [SageMaker Endpoint Deployment Summary](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/1692642280)

