## 7. Synthesis: Documentation Byproduct Taxonomy for the Skill

Based on the research above, here is the complete documentation byproduct taxonomy the ML Lifecycle Execution Stack skill should generate:

| Document Type | Trigger | Auto-Population Sources | Human Confirmation |
|---------------|---------|------------------------|-------------------|
| **Model Card** | Model registered to Tarot v3 | MLflow metadata, training params, eval metrics, Tecton features | Required (compliance) |
| **Experiment Report** | Experiment run completed | MLflow runs, evaluation results, hypothesis from Jira ticket | Required (decision field only) |
| **Architecture Comparison** | Multiple model versions evaluated | Parallel MLflow runs, cost data, latency benchmarks | Required (recommendation) |
| **Weekly Digest** | Scheduled (every Monday) | MLflow activity, Jira status, cost trends, drift alerts | Not required (auto-publish) |
| **PIR Draft** | Model deployment incident resolved | Incident timeline, model version, canary metrics, rollback history | Required (governance document) |
| **System Card** | New AI feature reaching production | All of the above + data flow documentation | Required (ISO certification) |

### Key Design Principles for AI Employee Implementation:

1. **Auto-populate from instrumentation**: Every document should be pre-filled from existing tool outputs (MLflow, Jira, monitoring systems) — the AI employee fills in structure, humans fill in judgment
2. **Progressive disclosure**: TL;DR → full detail — allow stakeholders to consume at appropriate depth
3. **Linkage**: Every document should link to source artifacts (MLflow run IDs, Jira tickets, Databricks tables)
4. **Versioning**: Documents should be versioned alongside the model they describe
5. **Decision gates**: Clearly separate autonomous documentation from documents requiring human approval
6. **Templated consistency**: Use standardized field schemas so documents are machine-parseable for future analysis

