## 7. Pattern: Continuous Calibration Feedback Loop (ORBIT Learning Integration)

### What it is
Every task completion feeds calibration data back into ORBIT, improving future confidence estimates. The "Learn" phase of the Symphony lifecycle must include calibration curve updates.

### How it works

```
Task Complete → Outcome Known↓
Compare (stated_confidence, actual_correctness)
        ↓
Update calibration curve for this domain
        ↓
If ECE > threshold: 
    → Tighten thresholds (more conservative)
    → Log calibration drift alert↓
ORBIT appends: {domain, confidence_band, accuracy, date}↓
Next agent bootstrapped with updated calibration offsets
```

### Metrics tracked per domain

| Metric | Definition | Target |
|---|---|---|
| **ECE** (Expected Calibration Error) | Average gap between confidence and accuracy across bins | < 5% |
| **Brier Score** | Mean squared difference between confidence and binary outcome | < 0.15 |
| **Overconfidence Rate** | % of high-confidence (>80%) predictions that are wrong | < 15% |
| **Abstention Rate** | % of tasks where agent declared "I don't know" | Track but don't minimize — some abstention is healthy |
| **False Autonomy Rate** | % of 🟢 decisions that should have been 🟡/🔴 | < 5% (safety-critical) |

### Source
- [RFC-002](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/AISDLC/pages/6849219616): Progression gates require "reliability curves, precision-at-threshold metrics" and "feedback loops to capture errors, overrides, production outcomes, and drift signals"
- [AI SDLC Code Review & Approval Vision](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/AISDLC/pages/6899989933): Backpressure model where component maturity (including calibration) rises and falls based on operational evidence
- [Gartner: How to Test Enterprise AI Agents](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/AnalystRelations/pages/5636530617): "Three-step testing framework that matches validation depth to risk level and allows agents to earn staged-autonomy through demonstrated competence"

